Sure, here's an example HTML page that includes the Swift code for checking if a number is prime and also allows the user to input a number and see if it is prime:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Prime Number Checker</title>
	<script type="text/swift">
		func isPrime(_ number: Int) -> Bool {
		    if number <= 1 {
		        return false
		    }

		    for i in 2..<number {
		        if number % i == 0 {
		            return false
		        }
		    }

		    return true
		}
	</script>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Prime Number Checker</h1>
	<p>Enter a number to see if it is prime:</p>
	<form onsubmit="event.preventDefault(); checkPrime()">
		<input type="number" id="inputNumber" required>
		<button type="submit">Check</button>
	</form>
	<p id="result"></p>
	<script>
		function checkPrime() {
			const number = parseInt(document.getElementById("inputNumber").value);
			const isPrimeResult = isPrime(number);
			const resultElement = document.getElementById("result");
			if (isPrimeResult) {
				resultElement.innerHTML = `${number} is prime!`;
			} else {
				resultElement.innerHTML = `${number} is not prime.`;
			}
		}
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```
This HTML page includes a form with an input field and a button for the user to input a number and check if it is prime. The `onsubmit` attribute of the form calls the `checkPrime()` function, which gets the value of the input field, passes it to the `isPrime()` function, and displays the result in the `result` paragraph element.
The `isPrime()` function checks if a given number is prime by iterating over all numbers from 2 to the number itself - 1. If any of those numbers divides the given number evenly (i.e., with a remainder of 0), then the given number is not prime. If none of the numbers divide the given number evenly, then the given number is prime.
To integrate the Swift code into the HTML page, we simply include it in a `<script type="text/swift">` tag in the `<head>` section of the page. We can then call the `isPrime()` function from a JavaScript function in the `<script>` tag in the `<body>` section of the page.

Here's an example C++ code to calculate the amount of money in a bank account with an initial deposit of $100 and an annual interest rate of 6%, compounded annually:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main() {
   double initialDeposit = 100.0;
   double annualInterestRate = 0.06;
   int years = 1;
   double amount = initialDeposit * pow(1 + annualInterestRate, years);
   cout << "After " << years << " years, the amount in the bank account is: $" << amount << endl;
   return 0;
}
```
This code initializes the initial deposit, annual interest rate, and number of years. It then calculates the amount in the bank account using the formula for compound interest, which is `amount = initialDeposit * pow(1 + annualInterestRate, years)`. Finally, it prints out the result to the console.

