To create a Scala code that sends a formal email with a secret code, you can use the JavaMail API. Here's an example code that you can modify to suit your needs:
```scala
import java.util.{Date, Properties}
import javax.mail.{Message, Session, Transport}
import javax.mail.internet.{InternetAddress, MimeMessage}
object EmailSender {
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    val recipient = "manager@example.com"
    val subject = "Request for Leave"
    val body = "Dear Manager,\n\nI would like to request for leave from [start date] to [end date]. Please let me know if there are any concerns.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]"
    // Generate secret code
    val dateFormat = new java.text.SimpleDateFormat("yyyyMMdd")
    val currentDate = dateFormat.format(new Date())
    val randomNum = scala.util.Random.nextInt(10000)
    val secretCode = s"$currentDate-$randomNum"
    // Set up mail properties
    val props = new Properties()
    props.put("mail.smtp.auth", "true")
    props.put("mail.smtp.starttls.enable", "true")
    props.put("mail.smtp.host", "smtp.gmail.com")
    props.put("mail.smtp.port", "587")
    // Set up mail session
    val session = Session.getInstance(props, new javax.mail.Authenticator() {
      override def getPasswordAuthentication(): javax.mail.PasswordAuthentication = {
        new javax.mail.PasswordAuthentication("your-email@example.com", "your-password")
      }
    })
    // Create message
    val message = new MimeMessage(session)
    message.setFrom(new InternetAddress("your-email@example.com"))
    message.setRecipients(Message.RecipientType.TO, InternetAddress.parse(recipient))
    message.setSubject(subject)
    message.setText(body + s"\n\nSecret code: $secretCode")
    // Send message
    Transport.send(message)
    println("Email sent successfully!")
  }
}
```
Make sure to replace "your-email@example.com" and "your-password" with your actual email address and password. Also, replace the placeholders in the "recipient", "subject", and "body" variables with your actual recipient's email address, subject, and message body.
Note that this code assumes that you're using a Gmail account to send the email. If you're using a different email provider, you may need to modify the mail properties accordingly.

Two lesser-known data types in JavaScript are Sets and Maps.
A Set is a collection of unique values where each value can occur only once. Here is an example of how to create a Set:
```
const mySet = new Set();
mySet.add("apple");
mySet.add("banana");
mySet.add("apple"); // This won't be added to the Set since "apple" is already in it
```
Sets are useful for removing duplicates from an array, checking if an element exists in a collection, and performing set operations like union, intersection, and difference. However, Sets are not indexed like arrays, so you cannot access elements by their position. 
A Map is a collection of key-value pairs where each key can map to only one value. Here is an example of how to create a Map:
```
const myMap = new Map();
myMap.set("apple", 1);
myMap.set("banana", 2);
```
Maps are useful for storing and retrieving data based on a specific key, and they can be iterated over using a for...of loop. However, Maps are not as efficient as plain objects for small collections of data.
Arrays and objects are more commonly used data types in JavaScript. Arrays are ordered collections of values that can be accessed using their index, while objects are unordered collections of key-value pairs. Arrays are useful for storing and manipulating lists of data, while objects are useful for representing complex data structures with named properties. However, arrays and objects can both contain duplicate values, unlike Sets and Maps.
In summary, Sets and Maps are useful for specific use cases where unique values or key-value pairs are needed. Arrays and objects are more general-purpose data types that can be used in a wide range of programming scenarios.

