Random Forest is a powerful machine learning algorithm that is used for classification and regression tasks. The benefits of using Random Forest in Go programming language include its ability to handle large datasets, its high accuracy, and its ability to handle missing data.
Here's a sample code demonstrating the implementation of Random Forest in Go:
```
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "github.com/sjwhitworth/golearn/base"
    "github.com/sjwhitworth/golearn/ensemble"
    "github.com/sjwhitworth/golearn/evaluation"
    "github.com/sjwhitworth/golearn/filters"
    "github.com/sjwhitworth/golearn/trees"
    "os"
)
func main() {
    // Load dataset
    rawData, err := base.ParseCSVToInstances("iris.csv", true)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        os.Exit(1)
    }
    // Filter out class attribute
    removeClass := filters.RemoveClass(rawData)
    removeClass.SpecifyClassAttribute(base.AttributeSpec{0})
    filteredData := base.NewLazilyFilteredInstances(removeClass)
    // Split dataset into training and testing sets
    trainData, testData := base.InstancesTrainTestSplit(filteredData, 0.5)
    // Create Random Forest classifier
    rf := ensemble.NewRandomForest(10, 3)
    rf.Fit(trainData)
    // Make predictions on testing set
    predictions := rf.Predict(testData)
    // Evaluate accuracy of predictions
    confusionMat, err := evaluation.GetConfusionMatrix(testData, predictions)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        os.Exit(1)
    }
    fmt.Println(evaluation.GetAccuracy(confusionMat))
}
```
In this code, we first load a dataset from a CSV file using the `base.ParseCSVToInstances` function. We then remove the class attribute from the dataset using the `filters.RemoveClass` function, and split the dataset into training and testing sets using the `base.InstancesTrainTestSplit` function.
Next, we create a Random Forest classifier using the `ensemble.NewRandomForest` function, and fit it to the training data using the `rf.Fit` method. We then use the `rf.Predict` method to make predictions on the testing set.
Finally, we evaluate the accuracy of the predictions using the `evaluation.GetConfusionMatrix` and `evaluation.GetAccuracy` functions.

