To modify the binary search algorithm to handle arrays with duplicate elements, we need to make some changes to the code. One approach is to use a modified binary search algorithm that first finds the leftmost occurrence of the target element, and then returns the index of the last occurrence of the target element. Here is an updated code that uses this approach:
```
func binarySearch(arr []int, target int) (int, int) {
    left := 0
    right := len(arr) - 1
    leftmost := -1
    rightmost := -1
    for left <= right {
        mid := (left + right) / 2
        if arr[mid] == target {
            leftmost = mid
            right = mid - 1
        } else if arr[mid] < target {
            left = mid + 1
        } else {
            right = mid - 1
        }
    }
    left = 0
    right = len(arr) - 1
    for left <= right {
        mid := (left + right) / 2
        if arr[mid] == target {
            rightmost = mid
            left = mid + 1
        } else if arr[mid] < target {
            left = mid + 1
        } else {
            right = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return leftmost, rightmost
}
```
In this code, we first initialize the leftmost and rightmost variables to -1. Then, we perform two binary searches. The first search finds the leftmost occurrence of the target element, and updates the leftmost variable accordingly. The second search finds the rightmost occurrence of the target element, and updates the rightmost variable accordingly.
Finally, we return both the leftmost and rightmost indexes of the target element. If the target element is not present in the array, both leftmost and rightmost will be -1.
Note that this approach assumes that the input array is sorted. If the array is not sorted, you will need to sort it first before using this algorithm.

