output:
This PHP code extracts the first common noun from a sentence, excluding proper nouns:
```
<?php
$sentence = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
// Remove punctuation and convert to lowercase
$words = explode(" ", strtolower(preg_replace("/[^A-Za-z0-9]/", " ", $sentence)));
// Filter out proper nouns and other non-nouns
$nouns = array_filter($words, function($word) {
    return (strpos($word, "_") === false && strpos($word, "-") === false && (substr($word, -1) == "s" ? in_array(substr($word, 0, -1), array("a", "an", "any", "each", "every", "many", "several", "these", "those")) : in_array($word, array("a", "an", "any", "each", "every", "many", "several", "these", "those", "i", "we", "you", "he", "she", "it", "they")) || preg_match("/^(.*[^s])s$/", $word)));
});
// Count occurrences of each noun and filter out single occurrences
$common_nouns = array_filter(array_count_values($nouns), function($count) {
    return $count > 1;
});
// Sort by frequency and get the first key
arsort($common_nouns);
$first_common_noun = array_key_first($common_nouns);
echo $first_common_noun; // Outputs "dog"
?>
```
The code first removes all punctuation and converts the sentence to lowercase. It then filters out proper nouns and other non-nouns, such as pronouns and articles. It then counts the occurrences of each noun and filters out those that only appear once. Finally, it sorts the remaining nouns by frequency and returns the first key, which is the first common noun. In this example, the output is "dog".

